    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d306-a1">

                <pb facs="1087" n="937" sameAs="frus:frus1907p2/pg937"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The general manager of the Panama
                            Railroad Company to Minister Squiers.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Panama Railroad Company,<lb/>Office of General
                                Manager</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Colon</hi></placeName>,
                            <date calendar="gregorian" when="1906-12-20"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December 20, 1906</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: It has been the custom for the Panama
                    Railroad Company for a number of years to have the settlement of the estates of
                    deceased employees made by the American consul at Colon. This method of
                    procedure is now being objected to by the present consul, as he claims
                    settlement should be made by Col. Tom M. Cooke, administrator of estates of the
                    Isthmian Canal Commission.</p>
                <pb facs="1088" n="938" xml:id="pg938"/>
                <p>We have never been informed that Mr. Cooke was to perform this function of making
                    settlement of the estates of deceased American employees of the railroad
                    company, and, if it can be done, I would be greatly obliged if the American
                    consul should be instructed to make settlements of the estates of railroad
                    employees as heretofore.</p>
                <p>My object in asking you to have this arrangement continued is that it is very
                    much more convenient for us, and I do not think that Mr. Cooke has ever received
                    instructions to take over the settlement of deceased railroad employees’
                    estates.</p>
                <p>Yours, truly,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. G. Bierd</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">General Manager.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
